// teko_time.tk -- `TimeSpan` and `DateTime`, C#'s own (docs/specs/datetime.md,
// crumbs C1 and C2): a signed 64-bit count of ticks, and a tick is 100
// nanoseconds; a date is a tick count from `0001-01-01 00:00:00` with a `Kind`
// in the two bits above it.
//
// Each type is ONE registration -- `type_new("TimeSpan", 8, 8, TK_SINT)`,
// `type_new("DateTime", 8, 8, TK_SINT)` --
// plus the two `syntax_expr`/`syntax_stmt` a type word needs to open an
// expression and a statement, which is exactly what a class name already gets
// (teko_access.tk's `tk_type_word`). Eight bytes wide is the word, so nothing
// narrows and nothing extends; the `TK_SINT` kind buys the signed comparison
// and the signed `/` the core already gives an `i64`, and costs no line in any
// machine (docs/specs/datetime.md § 1). It is deliberately kept OUT of
// `tk_is_int_ty` (teko_typeof.tk, D38's own rule: a `type_new` id joins that
// predicate only when the module that registered it says so by name, there) --
// a `TimeSpan` is not a number, and `f64 x = span;` is a refusal, not a
// widening.
//
// Everything else on this page is DATA: the member rows and the operator rows
// of teko_prim.tk's lowering table, each naming an ordinary function of
// `lib/time.tk` written over the raw `i64` ticks. No pass, no intrinsic, no
// `syntax*` of its own beyond the type word.
//
// `DateTime` IS ORDERED BY ITS TICKS AND NOT BY ITS BITS. C#'s own `dateData`
// packing puts the `Kind` in bits 62 and 63 (the maximum tick value,
// `3155378975999999999`, needs 62), so a `Local` date is a NEGATIVE `i64` and
// the core's own `<` over the raw eight bytes would answer backwards. That is
// why every comparison is a row -- a call into `lib/time.tk` that masks the
// two bits off -- and why `.Ticks` is a call here where `TimeSpan.Ticks` is
// the identity cast: the raw bits of a date are not its ticks.
//
// WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why: `.ToString()`/`Parse` are text, which lands with
// the `enum` text crumb's own machinery (docs/reference/not-yet.md);
// `TimeSpan * f64`, `TimeSpan / TimeSpan`, `DateTime.SpecifyKind` and
// `.Subtract` are C# members the spec's § 4/§ 6 leave out of these crumbs.
//
// `DateTime.Now`/`UtcNow`/`Today` and `DateTimeOffset.Now`/`UtcNow` DID need
// a wall clock, one symbol per host -- crumb C6, D90, below, landed it: the
// owner's ruling of 2026-09-08 is that the clock is teko's OWN `extern`, not
// an `mc` hook, so nothing there was ever actually blocked on `mc`. The stale
// claim otherwise, once in this file and once in `teko_prim.tk`'s own
// comment above `TK_PMSOON`, is what this crumb corrects.

i64 tk_ty_timespan = 0;
i64 tk_ty_datetime = 0;
i64 tk_ty_dateonly = 0;
i64 tk_ty_timeonly = 0;
i64 tk_ty_dto = 0;

// ============================================================================
// the wall clock (crumb C6, D90)
// ============================================================================
//
// `DateTime.Now`/`UtcNow`/`Today` and `DateTimeOffset.Now`/`UtcNow` need one
// wall-clock symbol per host: `clock_gettime` on Linux and macOS,
// `GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime` on Windows. `.github/workflows/ngen.yml`
// derives its five legs' configs from the SAME `teko.toml`, and its own
// comment states `[include]` "can never drift" between them -- so a quoted
// `#include "clock.tk"` cannot pick a different FILE per host, and declaring
// both wrappers unconditionally in `lib/time.tk` would leave an unresolved
// `extern` on whichever host does not own it: measured (a scratch `mc build`
// with a `call` to an undefined extern INSIDE a function nothing ever calls)
// -- `mc` does not dead-strip an unreachable function, so an unused wrapper's
// `extern` still has to resolve. WHERE it bites is the platform's: on
// macos/aarch64 the link itself succeeds (exit 0) and `dyld` binds eagerly,
// so the binary dies at process START, exit 134, `Symbol not found` --
// re-measured by the independent verifier, and worse than a link error, not
// better, since nothing in the build says a word about it.
//
// The mechanism that already solves "the same source, a different answer per
// host" is `#include <mc/host>` (`minicompiler/mc` src/core_bundle.mc): the
// bundle resolver behind every `#include <name>` is ONE function pointer
// (`lex_set_bundle`), and `host_bundle_open` -- what `core_teko.mc`'s own
// `mc_bundle_init()` installs -- already special-cases that one name to
// `host_include()`'s own answer, so a generated compiler is portable with no
// change to its two include lines. This wraps that SAME pointer for one more
// name, `<teko/clock.tk>`, and falls through to `host_bundle_open` for every
// other one, so `<sys>`, `<prelude>` and the rest of the bundle are
// untouched. That fallthrough calls `host_bundle_open` BY NAME rather than
// the pointer that was installed before this one, which is right while this
// is the only wrapper in the tree and wrong the day a second module wraps
// `lex_set_bundle` after `tk_clock_init()` -- the later wrapper would answer
// first and `<teko/clock.tk>` would silently stop resolving. A second wrapper
// has to chain the previous pointer instead. The OS is read `drv_os()` FIRST
// and `host_os()` only as its fallback: `ngen.yml`'s five legs are all NATIVE
// (docs/specs/roadmap-1.0.md, the owner's ruling of 2026-09-04) and there the
// two agree, but a CROSS build -- `mc build --config` with `[target] os =
// "windows"` on a macOS box -- has to emit the TARGET's wrapper, and only
// `drv_os()` knows it. `drv_os()` answers 0 on the bare-CLI path (no
// `[target]`; `src/driver.mc` zero-initialises the record), and there the
// host is the target, which is what the fallback is for.
//
// Each answer is a small, complete function, `tk_dt_raw_utc_ticks()`,
// returning C#'s own tick count (100 ns, since `0001-01-01`) read off the
// host clock -- `lib/time.tk`'s `Now`/`UtcNow` call nothing else. `rt_alloc`,
// `panic`, `ld64`, `UNIX_EPOCH_TICKS` and `TICKS_PER_SECOND` all read from
// `lib/time.tk`'s own scope at the point `#include <teko/clock.tk>` appears
// there, textually after `lib/rt.tk`'s include and the tick constants.
// CLOCK_REALTIME is 0 on both Linux and macOS. A negative return is the one
// failure `clock_gettime` documents (a bad clock id or an unmapped buffer,
// neither reachable through this call) -- panics rather than answering a
// wrong instant, exit 70, the road every other wall-clock read of this
// crumb takes.
uptr tk_clock_posix_src() {
    return "extern i32 clock_gettime(i32 clk, uptr tp);\ni64 tk_dt_raw_utc_ticks() {\n    uptr tp = rt_alloc(16);\n    i32 rc = clock_gettime(0, tp);\n    if (rc != 0) panic(\"the wall clock is not available\");\n    i64 sec = ld64(tp);\n    i64 nsec = ld64(tp + 8);\n    rt_free(tp, 16);\n    return UNIX_EPOCH_TICKS + sec * TICKS_PER_SECOND + nsec / 100;\n}\n";
}

// FILETIME: a 64-bit little-endian count of 100 ns ticks since `1601-01-01`,
// written as two 32-bit halves that `ld64` reads back as one -- the same
// trick every Windows program that aliases `FILETIME` to `ULARGE_INTEGER`
// relies on. The MSDN contract is that this call cannot fail, unlike the
// POSIX one. `FILETIME_EPOCH_DIFF_TICKS` is .NET's own constant, the tick
// count between `0001-01-01` and `1601-01-01`.
uptr tk_clock_windows_src() {
    return "extern void GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime(uptr tp);\nconst i64 FILETIME_EPOCH_DIFF_TICKS = 504911232000000000;\ni64 tk_dt_raw_utc_ticks() {\n    uptr tp = rt_alloc(8);\n    GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime(tp);\n    i64 ft = ld64(tp);\n    rt_free(tp, 8);\n    return ft + FILETIME_EPOCH_DIFF_TICKS;\n}\n";
}

// the wrapped bundle resolver: `<teko/clock.tk>` answered here, by host; every
// other name -- `<sys>`, `<prelude>`, `<mc/host>` itself -- handed straight
// to the resolver `mc_bundle_init()` already installed.
uptr tk_clock_bundle_open(uptr name, i64 base, uptr pcanon, uptr plen) {
    if (str_eq(name, "teko/clock.tk")) {
        // the TARGET's clock, not this machine's: `drv_os()` is what
        // `[target] os` (or `--os`) set, and a cross build -- `--os windows`
        // on a macOS box, which `docs/internals/bootstrap.md` discusses and
        // the ladder's own host guard refuses only for the LADDER -- must
        // emit the target's wrapper or link nothing. `drv_os()` answers 0 on
        // the bare-CLI path (no `[target]`, `src/driver.mc`'s record is
        // zero-initialised), and there the host IS the target, so `host_os()`
        // is the fallback rather than the rule (D90, amended after the
        // review).
        uptr os = drv_os();
        if (os == 0) os = host_os();
        uptr src = tk_clock_posix_src();
        if (str_eq(os, "windows")) src = tk_clock_windows_src();
        st64(pcanon, "teko/clock.tk");
        st64(plen, cstrlen(src));
        return src;
    }
    return host_bundle_open(name, base, pcanon, plen);
}

// Called once, from `tk_time_init()` below: installs the wrapper AFTER
// `mc_bundle_init()` (`core_teko.mc`'s `main()`) already ran, so
// `host_bundle_open` -- the pointer this wrapper falls through to -- exists.
void tk_clock_init() { lex_set_bundle(&tk_clock_bundle_open); }

// the include the lowering symbols live in, named in the refusal a program
// that forgot it gets (docs/specs/datetime.md § 9). BARE, since D76: the
// column carries no quote of its own anywhere in this table, and every
// consumer (`tk_prim_need_include_of`, `tw_ret`) quotes it through
// `tk_prim_inc_q` (teko_prim.tk) -- the one place that used to bake its own
// quotes in, which is exactly the defect D76 fixes.
uptr tk_time_include() { return "time.tk"; }

void tk_ts_statics() {
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    // the three values C# spells as static fields. Each is a call into
    // `lib/time.tk` rather than a folded literal: the surface list of
    // docs/specs/surface.md is closed by "every symbol has a body", and one
    // ordinary function per constant keeps every row of this table the same
    // shape.
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Zero",     TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_zero", ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "MinValue", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_min",  ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "MaxValue", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_max",  ts);
    // C#'s own five scale constants, on the type where C# puts them
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TicksPerDay",         TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_per_day", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TicksPerHour",        TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_per_hour", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TicksPerMinute",      TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_per_minute", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TicksPerSecond",      TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_per_second", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TicksPerMillisecond", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_per_ms", TY_I64);
    // the six builders. The five that take an `f64` are C#'s own `From*`
    // family, and an integer written at one of them converts on the spot
    // (`TimeSpan.FromHours(2)`, teko_prim.tk's `tk_prim_conv`).
    tk_prim_member(ts, "FromDays",         TK_PMSFUN, ty_f64, "tk_ts_from_days",    ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "FromHours",        TK_PMSFUN, ty_f64, "tk_ts_from_hours",   ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "FromMinutes",      TK_PMSFUN, ty_f64, "tk_ts_from_minutes", ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "FromSeconds",      TK_PMSFUN, ty_f64, "tk_ts_from_seconds", ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "FromMilliseconds", TK_PMSFUN, ty_f64, "tk_ts_from_ms",      ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "FromTicks",        TK_PMSFUN, TY_I64, "tk_ts_from_ticks",   ts);
}

void tk_ts_members() {
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    // `new TimeSpan(ticks)` and `.Ticks` are the two rows that are NOT calls:
    // they name the same eight bytes under the other type, so each is the
    // identity cast the compiler writes and the surface may not (§ 2). A row
    // with no symbol IS that identity.
    tk_prim_member(ts, "new",   TK_PMCTOR, TY_I64, 0, ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Ticks", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1,  0, TY_I64);
    // the components, C#'s own: `.Hours` is the hour of the span, 0..23, and
    // `.TotalHours` is the whole span as an f64. Both are asserted by the
    // fixture, because that is the difference users get wrong.
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Days",         TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_days",    TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Hours",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_hours",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Minutes",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_minutes", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Seconds",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_seconds", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Milliseconds", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_ms",      TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TotalDays",         TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_total_days",    ty_f64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TotalHours",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_total_hours",   ty_f64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TotalMinutes",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_total_minutes", ty_f64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TotalSeconds",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_total_seconds", ty_f64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "TotalMilliseconds", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_total_ms",      ty_f64);
    // the four methods
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Duration",  TK_PMFUN, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_duration", ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Negate",    TK_PMFUN, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_neg",      ts);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "CompareTo", TK_PMFUN, ts,    "tk_ts_cmp",      TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(ts, "Equals",    TK_PMFUN, ts,    "tk_ts_eq",       TY_I64);
}

// docs/specs/datetime.md § 4. Every row is a call, none is left to the core's
// own arithmetic: a raw `+` over two tick counts wraps where C# raises, and the
// P0 probe measured the core running `a * b` over two spans as an ordinary
// multiply. `t * n` and `n * t` share one function through the row's swap flag,
// which is what C#'s reversed `operator *(double, TimeSpan)` is here.
void tk_ts_operators() {
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    tk_prim_op(K_ADD, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_add", ts, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_sub", ts, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 1, ts, 0 - 1, "tk_ts_neg", ts, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_MUL, 2, ts, TY_I64, "tk_ts_mul", ts, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_MUL, 2, TY_I64, ts, "tk_ts_mul", ts, 1);
    tk_prim_op(K_DIV, 2, ts, TY_I64, "tk_ts_div", ts, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_EQ, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_eq", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_NE, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_ne", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LT, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_lt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LE, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_le", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GT, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_gt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GE, 2, ts, ts, "tk_ts_ge", TY_I64, 0);
}

// ---- `DateTime` (crumb C2) ----
// The statics, C#'s own. `UnixEpoch` carries `DateTimeKind.Utc` in its two top
// bits, exactly as C# declares it, which is why it is a call and not the bare
// tick constant -- `UnixEpoch.Ticks` still answers `621355968000000000`,
// because `.Ticks` masks the `Kind` off.
void tk_dt_statics() {
    i64 dt = tk_ty_datetime;
    tk_prim_member(dt, "MinValue",  TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_min",   dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "MaxValue",  TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_max",   dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "UnixEpoch", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_epoch", dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "IsLeapYear", TK_PMSFUN, TY_I64, "tk_dt_is_leap", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_membern(dt, "DaysInMonth", TK_PMSFUN, 2, TY_I64, "tk_dt_days_in_month", TY_I64);
    // C6, D90: the wall clock, `<teko/clock.tk>`'s own `tk_dt_raw_utc_ticks()`
    // read through three rows. `Now` carries `DateTimeKind.Local` -- C#'s own
    // spelling -- but teko has no time-zone database (§ 8, unmoved), so its
    // VALUE is the same clock read as `UtcNow`: an honest `Kind` on an
    // instant that is not actually converted, which is D90's own ruling and
    // is documented as exactly that, never silently. `Today` is `Now`
    // truncated to midnight, the same `Kind`.
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Now",    TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_now_local", dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "UtcNow", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_now_utc",   dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Today",  TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_today",     dt);
}

// The five constructors C# has that need no calendar the surface cannot
// spell, each a row of its own arity (`tk_prim_pick` is what tells them
// apart). NONE of them is the identity cast `TimeSpan`'s is: a tick count has
// to be checked against the range and packed with its `Kind`, and a date
// that does not exist -- `new DateTime(2023, 2, 29)` -- panics where it is
// built.
void tk_dt_ctors() {
    i64 dt = tk_ty_datetime;
    tk_prim_membern(dt, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 1, TY_I64, "tk_dt_from_ticks", dt);
    // the one row whose two arguments differ in TYPE: a tick count and a
    // `DateTimeKind`, the `enum` `lib/time.tk` declares (N2c). The type does
    // not exist here -- the include that declares it has not been read yet --
    // so the column names it and the site reads the id (`tk_prim_late`).
    tk_prim_member2(dt, "new", TK_PMCTOR, TY_I64, tk_prim_late("DateTimeKind"),
                    "tk_dt_from_ticks_kind", dt);
    tk_prim_membern(dt, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 3, TY_I64, "tk_dt_ymd", dt);
    tk_prim_membern(dt, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 6, TY_I64, "tk_dt_ymdhms", dt);
    tk_prim_membern(dt, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 7, TY_I64, "tk_dt_ymdhmsms", dt);
}

void tk_dt_members() {
    i64 dt = tk_ty_datetime;
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    // `.Ticks` is a CALL here, unlike `TimeSpan.Ticks`: the raw eight bytes
    // carry the `Kind` above the ticks, so reading them as a number is the one
    // thing that would be silently wrong. `.Kind` answers a `DateTimeKind`,
    // the `enum` of `lib/time.tk` (§ 1 as N2c rewrote it): the lowering symbol
    // still answers the underlying `i32` and `tk_prim_ret` casts the call to
    // the enum, so `i64 n = d.Kind;` is refused where the old alias converted.
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Ticks", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_ticks", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Kind",  TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_kind",  tk_prim_late("DateTimeKind"));
    // the civil calendar: proleptic Gregorian, computed as C# computes it
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Year",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_year",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Month",       TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_month",  TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Day",         TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_day",    TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Hour",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_hour",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Minute",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_minute", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Second",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_second", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Millisecond", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_ms",     TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "DayOfWeek",   TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_dow",    TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "DayOfYear",   TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_doy",    TY_I64);
    // the two that answer a value of another type: the date at midnight, and
    // the time since it
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Date",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_date", dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "TimeOfDay", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_tod",  ts);
    // C#'s `Add*` family: the five that take a `double` round to the nearest
    // millisecond (C#'s own `DateTime.Add(double, int)`), and the three that
    // take a whole count do not.
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddDays",         TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_dt_add_days",    dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddHours",        TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_dt_add_hours",   dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddMinutes",      TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_dt_add_minutes", dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddSeconds",      TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_dt_add_seconds", dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddMilliseconds", TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_dt_add_ms",      dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddTicks",  TK_PMFUN, TY_I64, "tk_dt_add_ticks",  dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddMonths", TK_PMFUN, TY_I64, "tk_dt_add_months", dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "AddYears",  TK_PMFUN, TY_I64, "tk_dt_add_years",  dt);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "CompareTo", TK_PMFUN, dt, "tk_dt_cmp", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dt, "Equals",    TK_PMFUN, dt, "tk_dt_eq",  TY_I64);
}

// docs/specs/datetime.md § 4, the rows that mix the two types. `d + t` and
// `t + d` are C#'s two declarations of one operator and share one function
// through the row's swap flag; `d + d` has NO row and is refused where it is
// written, which is the whole point of claiming every binary with a primitive
// operand.
void tk_dt_operators() {
    i64 dt = tk_ty_datetime;
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    tk_prim_op(K_ADD, 2, dt, ts, "tk_dt_add", dt, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_ADD, 2, ts, dt, "tk_dt_add", dt, 1);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 2, dt, ts, "tk_dt_sub_ts", dt, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_sub", ts, 0);
    // C#'s own `Compare`: the ticks, with the `Kind` masked off. Two dates of
    // different `Kind` compare by their ticks and do not convert.
    tk_prim_op(K_EQ, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_eq", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_NE, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_ne", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LT, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_lt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LE, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_le", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GT, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_gt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GE, 2, dt, dt, "tk_dt_ge", TY_I64, 0);
}

// ---- `DateTimeKind` is not registered here at all ----
// § 1 registered it as `type_alias("DateTimeKind", ty_i32)` plus a
// three-constant expression handler, because teko had no `enum` when the page
// was written. N2c (docs/specs/enum.md § 8) makes it what C# declares: an
// ORDINARY `enum` written in `lib/time.tk` beside the functions it belongs to,
// declared by the program's own `#include` like every other name that file
// carries. Nothing is registered for it in `tk_time_init()` -- the `enum`
// keyword's own handler (teko_enum.tk) does the `type_new` and the
// `syntax_expr`/`syntax_stmt` when the include is read -- and the rows above
// name it through `tk_prim_late`, which reads its id at the site.
//
// It is a TIGHTENING: an alias converts from any integer, an enum from nothing
// but itself, so `DateTimeKind k = 7;` and `i64 n = d.Kind;` are refused where
// they used to compile.

// ---- `TimeOnly` (crumb N4b) ----
// EIGHT bytes, and the value IS the ticks since midnight: an ordinary,
// non-negative `i64` in `0 .. TICKS_PER_DAY - 1` (`lib/time.tk`'s own
// `TIMEONLY_MAX_TICKS`). There is no `Kind` and no date above it, so
// `.Ticks` is the identity row `TimeSpan.Ticks` is, and the six comparisons
// are `TimeSpan`'s own functions -- a tick count of a time of day is an
// ordinary non-negative `i64`, exactly the case `DateOnly` already made
// (D54, and this crumb's own amendment to the spec's § 3).
//
// Registered BEFORE `DateOnly`: `DateOnly.ToDateTime(TimeOnly)`'s own column
// needs this id live, the same reason `DateOnly` itself is registered after
// `DateTime`.
void tk_to_statics() {
    i64 to = tk_ty_timeonly;
    tk_prim_member(to, "MinValue", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_to_min", to);
    tk_prim_member(to, "MaxValue", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_to_max", to);
    // the time of day half of a `DateTime`: `tk_dt_tod` already answers ticks
    // since midnight, masked and modulo `TICKS_PER_DAY` -- exactly a
    // `TimeOnly`'s own range, so it is named here directly and not copied.
    tk_prim_member(to, "FromDateTime", TK_PMSFUN, tk_ty_datetime, "tk_dt_tod", to);
    // a raw `TimeSpan`'s ticks, range-checked: the same guard `new
    // TimeOnly(ticks)` takes below, because both hand it one raw i64.
    tk_prim_member(to, "FromTimeSpan", TK_PMSFUN, tk_ty_timespan, "tk_to_from_ticks", to);
}

// the four constructors, each its own row of arity (`tk_prim_pick`): three
// calendar-free ones over `tk_dt_time_ticks` -- the same check
// `new DateTime(y, m, d, h, mi, s)` takes for its own time of day -- and the
// raw-ticks one, which needs its own range check because `tk_dt_time_ticks`
// only ever answers a value already inside range.
void tk_to_ctors() {
    i64 to = tk_ty_timeonly;
    tk_prim_membern(to, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 2, TY_I64, "tk_to_hm",    to);
    tk_prim_membern(to, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 3, TY_I64, "tk_to_hms",   to);
    tk_prim_membern(to, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 4, TY_I64, "tk_to_hmsms", to);
    tk_prim_membern(to, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 1, TY_I64, "tk_to_from_ticks", to);
}

void tk_to_members() {
    i64 to = tk_ty_timeonly;
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    // the identity row: the eight bytes under the other type, no call
    tk_prim_member(to, "Ticks", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, 0, TY_I64);
    // the components, reached through `DateTime`'s own: a tick count of a
    // TIME OF DAY is exactly what those functions already mask for, and a
    // `TimeOnly` carries no `Kind` to mask off in the first place -- the mask
    // is a no-op over a value already inside `0 .. TICKS_PER_DAY - 1`.
    tk_prim_member(to, "Hour",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_hour",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(to, "Minute",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_minute", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(to, "Second",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_second", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(to, "Millisecond", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dt_ms",     TY_I64);
    // C#'s wrapping `Add*` family: `Add` never panics, it wraps at both ends
    // of the day, and `AddHours`/`AddMinutes` are C#'s own form,
    // `Add(TimeSpan.FromHours(v))`.
    tk_prim_member(to, "Add",        TK_PMFUN, ts,    "tk_to_add",         to);
    tk_prim_member(to, "AddHours",   TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_to_add_hours",   to);
    tk_prim_member(to, "AddMinutes", TK_PMFUN, ty_f64, "tk_to_add_minutes", to);
    // the identity the other way: the same ticks under `TimeSpan`, no call
    tk_prim_member(to, "ToTimeSpan", TK_PMFUN, 0 - 1, 0, ts);
    tk_prim_member2(to, "IsBetween", TK_PMFUN, to, to, "tk_to_is_between", TY_I64);
    // ...and the two methods, the `TimeSpan`/`DateOnly` functions again: a
    // tick count of a time of day compares exactly like one of a day number.
    tk_prim_member(to, "CompareTo", TK_PMFUN, to, "tk_ts_cmp", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(to, "Equals",    TK_PMFUN, to, "tk_ts_eq",  TY_I64);
}

// The six comparisons, `TimeSpan`'s own functions for the reason `CompareTo`
// is above, and ONE arithmetic operator: `t - t`, which C# answers as the
// ELAPSED time and never as a negative `TimeSpan` (`tk_to_sub`,
// `lib/time.tk`). `t + t` has no row and is refused where it is written --
// C# declares no such operator -- and neither does `d + t`/`d - d` gain one
// here: those stay `DateOnly`'s own refusal, `tk_do_operators` below.
void tk_to_operators() {
    i64 to = tk_ty_timeonly;
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    tk_prim_op(K_EQ, 2, to, to, "tk_ts_eq", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_NE, 2, to, to, "tk_ts_ne", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LT, 2, to, to, "tk_ts_lt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LE, 2, to, to, "tk_ts_le", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GT, 2, to, to, "tk_ts_gt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GE, 2, to, to, "tk_ts_ge", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 2, to, to, "tk_to_sub", ts, 0);
}

// ---- `DateOnly` (crumb N4a) ----
// FOUR bytes, not eight, and C#'s own representation: the value IS the day
// number since `0001-01-01`, an `int` there and a width-4 `TK_SINT` here. That
// is the one thing this type brings that `TimeSpan` and `DateTime` did not --
// the compiler's own two casts are a sign extension in and a narrowing store
// out (teko_prim.tk § "the two casts") -- and it costs no line anywhere else:
// an `enum : i32` already travels every slot a value has (`DateTimeKind`,
// lib/time.tk), and `tests/surface_dateonly.tk` walks the same set for this
// one.
//
// There is no `Kind` above the number, so the raw four bytes ARE `.DayNumber`
// and that row carries no symbol: it is the identity cast `TimeSpan.Ticks` is.
void tk_do_statics() {
    i64 dy = tk_ty_dateonly;
    tk_prim_member(dy, "MinValue", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_do_min", dy);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "MaxValue", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_do_max", dy);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "FromDayNumber", TK_PMSFUN, TY_I64, "tk_do_from_daynum", dy);
    // the one column naming ANOTHER primitive, which is why `DateOnly` is
    // registered after `DateTime`: the id has to be live when the row is
    // written. The site casts the argument to its raw `i64` (`tk_prim_conv`),
    // exactly as it does for the receiver of an instance row.
    tk_prim_member(dy, "FromDateTime", TK_PMSFUN, tk_ty_datetime, "tk_do_from_datetime", dy);
}

void tk_do_members() {
    i64 dy = tk_ty_dateonly;
    i64 dt = tk_ty_datetime;
    tk_prim_membern(dy, "new", TK_PMCTOR, 3, TY_I64, "tk_do_ymd", dy);
    // the identity row: the four bytes under the other type, no call
    tk_prim_member(dy, "DayNumber", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, 0, TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "Year",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_do_year",  TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "Month",     TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_do_month", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "Day",       TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_do_day",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "DayOfWeek", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_do_dow",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "DayOfYear", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_do_doy",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "AddDays",   TK_PMFUN, TY_I64, "tk_do_add_days",   dy);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "AddMonths", TK_PMFUN, TY_I64, "tk_do_add_months", dy);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "AddYears",  TK_PMFUN, TY_I64, "tk_do_add_years",  dy);
    // ...and the two that compare, which are the `TimeSpan` functions and not
    // two wrappers around them: a day number is an ordinary non-negative
    // `i64`, so `tk_ts_cmp` and `tk_ts_eq` already answer exactly this.
    tk_prim_member(dy, "CompareTo", TK_PMFUN, dy, "tk_ts_cmp", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(dy, "Equals",    TK_PMFUN, dy, "tk_ts_eq",  TY_I64);
    // the member N4a left out because the argument's type did not exist yet
    // (docs/specs/datetime-extras.md § 4): the day number back into ticks,
    // plus the time of day, `Kind` Unspecified -- the id is LIVE here because
    // `TimeOnly` is registered before `DateOnly` (`tk_time_init`, below).
    tk_prim_member(dy, "ToDateTime", TK_PMFUN, tk_ty_timeonly, "tk_do_to_datetime", dt);
}

// The six comparisons and NOTHING else: C# declares no arithmetic operator on
// a `DateOnly`, so `d + t` and `d - d` have no row and are refused where they
// are written; `d1.DayNumber - d2.DayNumber` is the form, there and here.
// Every row names a `tk_ts_*` function, for the reason `CompareTo` does.
void tk_do_operators() {
    i64 dy = tk_ty_dateonly;
    tk_prim_op(K_EQ, 2, dy, dy, "tk_ts_eq", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_NE, 2, dy, dy, "tk_ts_ne", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LT, 2, dy, dy, "tk_ts_lt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LE, 2, dy, dy, "tk_ts_le", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GT, 2, dy, dy, "tk_ts_gt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GE, 2, dy, dy, "tk_ts_ge", TY_I64, 0);
}

// ---- `DateTimeOffset` (crumb N5, D76) ----
// SIXTEEN bytes, the third `TK_WIDE` type after `decimal` and `Guid`
// (`teko_wide.tk`, D74/D75): `+0` a `DateTime` whose `Kind` is ALWAYS `Utc`
// (the instant), `+8` the offset in SIGNED MINUTES. Registered LAST in
// `tk_time_init()`, below, so `tk_ty_datetime` and `tk_ty_timespan` are live
// ids when its constructor and operator rows name them -- neither is a
// `tk_prim_late` column, because neither is declared by an include this file
// has not read yet, unlike `DateTimeKind`.
//
// Its receiver and its VALUE both cross by address, D75's own rule for a wide
// type (`tk_prim_conv`/`tk_prim_emit`, teko_prim.tk): every row below names a
// symbol of `lib/time.tk` that takes and answers the `DateTimeOffset` struct
// itself, never a raw `i64` -- unlike `TimeSpan`/`DateTime`, whose eight bytes
// cross under the compiler's own cast. `DateTime`/`TimeSpan` ARGUMENTS
// (the constructor's first argument, `o + ts`'s second) still cross raw,
// because those columns name an EIGHT-byte primitive and `tk_prim_conv`
// answers that question by the COLUMN's own type, not by the row's.
void tk_dto_statics() {
    i64 o = tk_ty_dto;
    tk_prim_member(o, "MinValue",  TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_min",       o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "MaxValue",  TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_max",       o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "UnixEpoch", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_unixepoch", o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "FromUnixTimeSeconds",      TK_PMSFUN, TY_I64, "tk_dto_from_unix_seconds", o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "FromUnixTimeMilliseconds", TK_PMSFUN, TY_I64, "tk_dto_from_unix_ms",      o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Parse", TK_PMSFUN, TY_UPTR, "tk_dto_parse", o);
    // The row below never emits: `DateTimeOffset.TryParse(s, out o)` is
    // hand-parsed (`tk_dto_expr`, below), exactly as `Guid.TryParse` is
    // (D75) -- `out <name>` is no column the `pmr_*` table has. It is
    // registered all the same, so an instance access answers "static; reach
    // it through its type" rather than "unknown member".
    tk_prim_membern(o, "TryParse", TK_PMSFUN, 2, TY_UPTR, "tk_dto_tryparse", TY_I64);
    // C6, D90: the same clock `DateTime.Now`/`UtcNow` read. Offset `+0`,
    // C#'s own for `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`; `Now`'s offset is ALSO `+0` here
    // -- teko has no time-zone database (Ruling 7, § 8, unmoved), so the two
    // are the one instant under two names, exactly as `DateTime.Now`/`UtcNow`
    // now are, and for the same documented reason.
    tk_prim_member(o, "Now",    TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_now",     o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "UtcNow", TK_PMSVAL, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_now_utc", o);
}

// Ruling 1: ONE two-argument constructor, `new DateTimeOffset(DateTime,
// TimeSpan)`. C#'s `(i64 ticks, TimeSpan)` overload is NOT taught -- there is
// no second row of arity 2 to tell it from, `tk_prim_pick` picks a row by
// COUNT alone, and `new DateTimeOffset(new DateTime(t), ts)` is the written
// form (docs/reference/not-yet.md).
void tk_dto_ctors() {
    tk_prim_member2(tk_ty_dto, "new", TK_PMCTOR, tk_ty_datetime, tk_ty_timespan,
                    "tk_dto_new", tk_ty_dto);
}

void tk_dto_members() {
    i64 o = tk_ty_dto;
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    i64 dt = tk_ty_datetime;
    tk_prim_member(o, "Offset",             TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_offset", ts);
    tk_prim_member(o, "TotalOffsetMinutes", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_offmin", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "UtcDateTime",   TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_utcdatetime",   dt);
    // `.DateTime` and `.LocalDateTime` are one function under two names: teko
    // has no time-zone database, so "local" means "the offset this value
    // carries" and nothing more (§ 4's own amendment).
    tk_prim_member(o, "LocalDateTime", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_localdatetime", dt);
    tk_prim_member(o, "DateTime",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_localdatetime", dt);
    // the components, of the LOCAL reading (§ 4's own table)
    tk_prim_member(o, "Year",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_year",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Month",       TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_month",  TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Day",         TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_day",    TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Hour",        TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_hour",   TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Minute",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_minute", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Second",      TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_second", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Millisecond", TK_PMPROP, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_ms",     TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "ToUnixTimeSeconds",      TK_PMFUN, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_to_unix_seconds", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "ToUnixTimeMilliseconds", TK_PMFUN, 0 - 1, "tk_dto_to_unix_ms",      TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "ToOffset",  TK_PMFUN, ts, "tk_dto_to_offset", o);
    tk_prim_member(o, "CompareTo", TK_PMFUN, o,  "tk_dto_cmp", TY_I64);
    tk_prim_member(o, "Equals",    TK_PMFUN, o,  "tk_dto_eq",  TY_I64);
    // the two `ToString` rows are one overload set told apart by the number
    // of arguments written (`tk_prim_pick`), the same way `Guid`'s are
    tk_prim_member(o, "ToString", TK_PMFUN, 0 - 1,   "tk_dto_tostring",     TY_UPTR);
    tk_prim_member(o, "ToString", TK_PMFUN, TY_UPTR, "tk_dto_tostring_fmt", TY_UPTR);
}

// Ruling: ordering (`==` included) compares the INSTANT only, offset ignored
// -- C#'s own rule, § 3, and the reason `tk_dto_eq`/`tk_dto_cmp` read
// `tk_dto_instant` and never `tk_dto_offmin`.
void tk_dto_operators() {
    i64 o = tk_ty_dto;
    i64 ts = tk_ty_timespan;
    tk_prim_op(K_EQ, 2, o, o, "tk_dto_eq", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_NE, 2, o, o, "tk_dto_ne", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LT, 2, o, o, "tk_dto_lt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_LE, 2, o, o, "tk_dto_le", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GT, 2, o, o, "tk_dto_gt", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_GE, 2, o, o, "tk_dto_ge", TY_I64, 0);
    tk_prim_op(K_ADD, 2, o, ts, "tk_dto_add",    o,  0);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 2, o, ts, "tk_dto_sub_ts", o,  0);
    tk_prim_op(K_SUB, 2, o, o,  "tk_dto_sub",    ts, 0);
}

// `DateTimeOffset.TryParse(s, out o)`, parsed by hand -- `teko_guid.tk`'s own
// shape and reason (D75): `out o` is read directly through `tk_ref_addr`
// (teko_ref.tk), tagged (`tk_rfarg_tag`) so `teko_ref.tk`'s own pass judges
// the target -- a global's declared type, a `ref`/`out` parameter repassed as
// the caller's own slot -- exactly as `Guid.TryParse`'s does.
i64 tk_dto_tryparse_expr(i64 line, uptr fl) {
    tk_prim_need_include(tk_ty_dto, "tk_dto_tryparse", line, fl);
    p_expect(K_LPAR, "expected ( after TryParse");
    i64 se = parse_expr(0);
    p_expect(K_COMMA, "expected , after the string");
    if (type_of_token(p_id()) != tk_ty_out)
        err_at(fl, line, "teko: TryParse's second argument is `out <name>`");
    p_next();
    i64 pty = 0 - 1;
    i64 oe = tk_ref_addr(line, fl, &pty);
    p_expect(K_RPAR, "expected ) after TryParse");
    if (pty >= 0 && pty != tk_ty_dto)
        tk_reject_compat(tk_pointee_name(pty), "DateTimeOffset", line, fl);
    tk_rfarg_tag(oe, TK_RP_OUT, pty);
    tk_line = line;
    tk_file = fl;
    return tk_call2("tk_dto_tryparse", se, oe);
}

// the type word in EXPRESSION position: this file's own handler, for the one
// hand-parsed static above -- every other member goes straight to the table
// (`tk_prim_static_m`, with the member name already read).
i64 tk_dto_expr() {
    i64 line = p_line();
    uptr fl = p_file();
    p_next();                                    // the type word
    if (!p_accept(tk_dot_tok))
        err_at2(fl, line, "teko: a type name reaches its static members", "DateTimeOffset");
    uptr m = tk_member_name();                   // D68
    if (str_eq(m, "TryParse")) return tk_dto_tryparse_expr(line, fl);
    return tk_prim_static_m(tk_ty_dto, m, line, fl);
}

// Called from teko.tk's `teko_init()` AFTER `tk_float_init()`: the `From*` and
// `Add*` rows name `ty_f64`, the id `<float>`'s own registration returns, so
// the float library has to be wired before this table is written.
void tk_time_init() {
    tk_clock_init();

    tk_ty_timespan = type_new("TimeSpan", 8, 8, TK_SINT);
    syntax_expr("TimeSpan", &tk_prim_expr);
    syntax_stmt("TimeSpan", &tk_prim_stmt);
    tk_prim_type(tk_ty_timespan, "TimeSpan", tk_time_include(), "`.Ticks` reads it", "`new TimeSpan(...)` builds it", 0);
    tk_ts_statics();
    tk_ts_members();
    tk_ts_operators();

    tk_ty_datetime = type_new("DateTime", 8, 8, TK_SINT);
    syntax_expr("DateTime", &tk_prim_expr);
    syntax_stmt("DateTime", &tk_prim_stmt);
    tk_prim_type(tk_ty_datetime, "DateTime", tk_time_include(), "`.Ticks` reads it", "`new DateTime(...)` builds it", 0);
    tk_dt_statics();
    tk_dt_ctors();
    tk_dt_members();
    tk_dt_operators();

    // AFTER `DateTime`, BEFORE `DateOnly`: `TimeOnly.FromDateTime(d)`'s own
    // column carries `DateTime`'s id, and `DateOnly.ToDateTime(t)`'s own
    // column below carries THIS one -- both read where they are written and
    // neither is late by name (`tk_prim_late` is for a type the very include
    // declares, not one this same function has already registered).
    tk_ty_timeonly = type_new("TimeOnly", 8, 8, TK_SINT);
    syntax_expr("TimeOnly", &tk_prim_expr);
    syntax_stmt("TimeOnly", &tk_prim_stmt);
    tk_prim_type(tk_ty_timeonly, "TimeOnly", tk_time_include(), "`.Ticks` reads it", "`new TimeOnly(...)` builds it", 0);
    tk_to_statics();
    tk_to_ctors();
    tk_to_members();
    tk_to_operators();

    // AFTER `DateTime` AND `TimeOnly`: `DateOnly.FromDateTime(d)`'s and
    // `.ToDateTime(t)`'s own columns carry those ids, and a column is read
    // where it is written and not late by name (`tk_prim_late` is for a type
    // the very include declares).
    tk_ty_dateonly = type_new("DateOnly", 4, 4, TK_SINT);
    syntax_expr("DateOnly", &tk_prim_expr);
    syntax_stmt("DateOnly", &tk_prim_stmt);
    tk_prim_type(tk_ty_dateonly, "DateOnly", tk_time_include(), "`.DayNumber` reads it", "`new DateOnly(...)` builds it", 0);
    tk_do_statics();
    tk_do_members();
    tk_do_operators();

    // LAST: `DateTimeOffset`'s columns name `DateTime` and `TimeSpan`, both
    // live ids by now (Ruling 4). The third `TK_WIDE` type after `decimal`
    // and `Guid` -- `tk_wide_init()` already ran in `teko_init()`
    // (teko.tk, before `tk_guid_init()`, before this function), so
    // `tk_wide_add` has a machine to register into.
    tk_ty_dto = type_new("DateTimeOffset", 16, 16, TK_WIDE);
    tk_wide_add(tk_ty_dto, "tk_dto_ld", "tk_dto_st", "tk_dto_retbuf");
    syntax_expr("DateTimeOffset", &tk_dto_expr);
    syntax_stmt("DateTimeOffset", &tk_prim_stmt);
    tk_prim_type(tk_ty_dto, "DateTimeOffset", tk_time_include(),
                 "`.UtcDateTime` reads it", "`new DateTimeOffset(...)` builds it",
                 "DateTimeOffset.MinValue");
    tk_dto_statics();
    tk_dto_ctors();
    tk_dto_members();
    tk_dto_operators();
}
